WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.866
The warehouse's cleaning crew has a problem of scale.

2
00:00:05.866 --> 00:00:11.835
The floor holds thousands of boxes, and checking every one of them on every round would take all day.

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.108
But the crew has noticed something.

4
00:00:14.108 --> 00:00:21.938
Near the door sit the new-arrivals shelves, and most boxes there are thrown out within minutes: packing, wrapping, delivery slips.

5
00:00:21.938 --> 00:00:23.926
Long-term storage hardly changes.

6
00:00:23.926 --> 00:00:26.576
So the crew looks hardest where the mess is.

7
00:00:27.467 --> 00:00:30.938
Which brings up a question that's easy to be shy about.

8
00:00:30.938 --> 00:00:34.789
A collection that frees more garbage must take longer, right?

9
00:00:34.789 --> 00:00:36.304
More rubbish, more work?

10
00:00:36.304 --> 00:00:37.819
Hold on to that thought.

11
00:00:38.933 --> 00:00:43.226
Let's watch it in the lesson's running example, a thumbnail service.

12
00:00:43.226 --> 00:00:51.623
One request allocates a lot, for a moment: the uploaded bytes, the decoded picture, the small thumbnail, a few strings, the response.

13
00:00:51.623 --> 00:00:54.591
A few milliseconds later, all of it is garbage.

14
00:00:54.733 --> 00:01:00.436
Only a few objects live long: the cache of recent thumbnails, and the service's settings.

15
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.602
That split has a name: the weak generational hypothesis.

16
00:01:04.602 --> 00:01:06.245
Most objects die young.

17
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.945
How lopsided is it?

18
00:01:08.945 --> 00:01:19.781
In one JDK 25 run of the lesson's stand-in service, the heap held 106 MB before a young collection, and 8 MB after it.

19
00:01:19.781 --> 00:01:22.260
98 of the 106 were garbage.

20
00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:26.190
So HotSpot's collectors split the heap to match.

21
00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:29.437
New objects go into eden, the new-arrivals shelves.

22
00:01:29.437 --> 00:01:31.793
Eden belongs to the young generation.

23
00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:40.707
When eden fills, a young collection finds the few live objects there, and copies them into a survivor space, a small short-stay shelf.

24
00:01:40.707 --> 00:01:43.653
Everything else in eden is reclaimed at once.

25
00:01:43.653 --> 00:01:46.076
The collector never even looks at it.

26
00:01:46.867 --> 00:01:47.697
Let's map it.

27
00:01:47.697 --> 00:01:49.867
The new-arrivals shelves are eden.

28
00:01:49.867 --> 00:01:52.485
The short-stay shelf is a survivor space.

29
00:01:52.485 --> 00:01:55.039
Long-term storage is the old generation.

30
00:01:55.039 --> 00:01:59.380
And one quick round of the crew near the door is a young collection.

31
00:02:00.267 --> 00:02:03.931
Each time an object survives, its age goes up by one.

32
00:02:03.931 --> 00:02:10.292
The age lives in 4 bits of the mark word, the housekeeping strip on the label from lesson 1.

33
00:02:10.292 --> 00:02:12.020
Four bits count up to 15.

34
00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:19.738
After enough survivals, the object is promoted: copied into the old generation, which is collected much less often.

35
00:02:19.738 --> 00:02:23.212
The age where that happens is the tenuring threshold.

36
00:02:23.212 --> 00:02:29.308
It's at most 15, and G1, the default collector, lowers it when the survivor space runs short.

37
00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:33.861
Here's the demo's age table, trimmed.

38
00:02:33.861 --> 00:02:37.324
At GC 0, 1,755,920 bytes are age 1.

39
00:02:37.324 --> 00:02:41.678
What happens to them at the next collection?

40
00:02:41.678 --> 00:02:43.261
Pause and guess.

41
00:02:46.267 --> 00:02:50.135
At GC 1, 1,754,832 bytes are age 2.

42
00:02:50.135 --> 00:02:53.894
At GC 2, the same bytes are age 3.

43
00:02:53.894 --> 00:02:59.641
The same cache entries, one birthday per collection.

44
00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:04.899
By GC 3, they no longer fit the 8 MB of survivor space G1 wanted.

45
00:03:04.899 --> 00:03:08.978
So G1 dropped the threshold to 2, and promoted them.

46
00:03:08.978 --> 00:03:12.508
The old generation grew from 2 regions to 10.

47
00:03:12.508 --> 00:03:16.744
G1 counts its heap in regions, equal blocks of memory.

48
00:03:17.667 --> 00:03:21.933
Now here the warehouse picture breaks, and the break is the key idea.

49
00:03:21.933 --> 00:03:26.448
A real crew leaves a wanted box where it is, and carries out the rubbish.

50
00:03:26.448 --> 00:03:28.797
A copying collector does the opposite.

51
00:03:28.797 --> 00:03:33.373
It carries out every box it keeps, and then declares the whole area empty.

52
00:03:34.267 --> 00:03:40.932
It's like moving house by carrying out only the things you love, and leaving everything else for the bulldozer.

53
00:03:40.932 --> 00:03:42.434
You never touch the junk.

54
00:03:42.434 --> 00:03:45.316
So the point is: the work follows what you keep.

55
00:03:46.267 --> 00:03:48.430
So, back to our question.

56
00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:50.506
More garbage, more work?

57
00:03:50.506 --> 00:03:50.766
No.

58
00:03:50.766 --> 00:03:55.697
Two young collections each start with a full 500 MB eden.

59
00:03:55.697 --> 00:03:57.514
In one, 5 MB survive.

60
00:03:57.514 --> 00:03:59.331
In the other, 200 MB.

61
00:03:59.331 --> 00:04:01.234
Which pause is longer?

62
00:04:01.234 --> 00:04:02.619
Pause and guess.

63
00:04:05.667 --> 00:04:06.382
The second.

64
00:04:06.382 --> 00:04:10.547
It copies forty times as much, while the program's threads wait.

65
00:04:10.547 --> 00:04:14.647
A young collection costs what survives, not what was allocated.

66
00:04:15.533 --> 00:04:19.735
Which makes one shape of object the expensive kind: medium-lived.

67
00:04:19.735 --> 00:04:29.948
It survives a few young collections, gets copied each time, gets promoted, and then dies in the old generation, where only a bigger collection can reclaim it.

68
00:04:29.948 --> 00:04:34.796
A thumbnail cache whose entries live for ten minutes is exactly that shape.

69
00:04:35.667 --> 00:04:37.072
So the rule is short.

70
00:04:37.072 --> 00:04:39.214
Let temporary objects die young.

71
00:04:39.214 --> 00:04:41.489
What you keep is what you pay for.

72
00:04:41.667 --> 00:04:43.805
Most boxes are thrown out young.

73
00:04:43.805 --> 00:04:54.096
So the JVM gives them their own shelves, copies out the few survivors, counts their birthdays on the label, and moves the old-timers to long-term storage.

74
00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:55.768
Quick check.

75
00:04:55.768 --> 00:05:00.631
A service keeps each request's data in a list for one minute, then drops it.

76
00:05:00.631 --> 00:05:04.087
Is that data short-lived, medium-lived, or long-lived?

77
00:05:07.333 --> 00:05:08.173
Medium-lived.

78
00:05:08.173 --> 00:05:15.603
Each entry gets copied through the survivor space, promoted, and later has to be cleaned out of the old generation.
